  "overview": {
    "text": "Cherenkov radiation is electromagnetic light produced when a charged particle moves through a transparent material faster than light’s phase speed in that material—not faster than light in a vacuum. The particle disturbs the material, and the resulting light waves form a forward-facing cone, somewhat like a sonic boom’s shock front. In water around a reactor, this can appear as a blue or violet glow. Because the cone’s angle depends on the particle’s speed, detectors can use the light to help measure speed and identify particles. Pavel Cherenkov observed the effect in 1934; Ilya Frank and Igor Tamm later explained it theoretically, and all three shared the 1958 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/cherenkov-radiation-explained",
      "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v2/91",
      "https://home.cern/how-detector-works/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "The effect occurs when a charged particle travels faster than light does in a particular transparent material; it does not mean the particle exceeds light’s speed in a vacuum.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/cherenkov-radiation-explained",
        "https://www.fnal.gov/pub/science/inquiring/matter/smallest/detection.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The emitted radiation forms a cone, and its angle depends on the particle’s speed.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v2/91",
        "https://home.cern/how-detector-works/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The glow around a reactor can look blue or violet; the exact visible appearance is not the definition of the effect.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/cherenkov-radiation-explained",
        "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v2/91"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cherenkov detectors use the emitted light to help measure particle speed and identify particles.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://home.cern/how-detector-works/",
        "https://www.fnal.gov/pub/science/inquiring/matter/smallest/detection.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pavel Cherenkov observed the effect in 1934; Ilya Frank and Igor Tamm later explained it, and the three received the 1958 Nobel Prize in Physics for their work.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/cherenkov-radiation-explained"
      ]
    }
  ],
